Ladybird now applies the textLength of every text and tspan element,
children before parents, with a resolved descendant counted as one
typographic character of its ancestor, as the spec’s algorithm says.
Blink does so in SvgTextLayoutAlgorithm::ApplyTextLengthAttribute().
Run in Chrome 158 with Ahem, 8 of 10 cases give the same character
positions in both engines, including text after an expanded or a
squeezed tspan, spacingAndGlyphs, an x or a dx inside the tspan, and
nested tspans. In the other two, Chrome lets the outer element’s
spacing change a nested tspan’s length, which the spec doesn’t allow;
Ladybird keeps it. Gecko reads textLength from the text element only,
in SVGTextFrame::DoGlyphPositioning(), so Firefox 151 ignores it on a
tspan. And WebKit 26.5 applies it to a tspan only partly.
